1. Audit RUN66 Artin proof: agree, and its factor 2 can be removed
from this local identity under the actual component hypothesis.

Write G=Gal(Qbar/Q), H=Gal(Qbar/Q(mu_{Nm})), chi=cyc^e psi,
x a component cocycle with action chi^{-1}, m x=0. Because H fixes
mu_{Nm}, its action is trivial on m-torsion (including m not a pure
ell-power); consequently x|H is an additive homomorphism and
    x(s g s^{-1}) = chi(s)^{-1} x(g)       (g in H).
The cocycle expansion cancels both x(s) terms exactly. No
assumption of invariance on all of G.

At ell, take delta in inertia at the actual place A with
chi(delta)=a^e by exists_inertia_cycPowDirichlet_eq. For g in H
intersect D_A, the commutator delta^{-1} g delta g^{-1} is
in D_A, in H by normality, and has FULL ell-adic cyclotomic
character 1 because that character is abelian. Thus it lies in
cycloGalGroup N intersect shaLocalSet ell Q 1, precisely the
source vanishing set (nonzero index 1, not the e=0 inertia case).
Its x-value is (a^e-1)x(g). Hence A0 x|H kills all local
decomposition groups over ell, where A0=a^e-1.

At finite places away from ell, x|H vanishes on inertia.
At infinity, if K=Q(mu_{Nm}) is totally imaginary there is no
nontrivial local Artin image. Otherwise each real decomposition
group of H is generated by a conjugate s c s^{-1} of the selected
complex conjugation c. Such a conjugate lies in H, so c also lies
in H; the preceding conjugation identity and x(c)=0 give
x(s c s^{-1})=0. This uses the actual normalization x(c)=0 from
Components, NOT merely that c has order 2. Thus A0 x already
kills the infinite contributions. RUN66's additional factor 2
is sufficient but unnecessary here. This is a human strengthening,
not a claim that the native lemma's statement was changed.

The continuous homomorphism f=A0 x|H has finite image since H is
compact and the coefficient group is finite/discrete. Its kernel
defines a finite abelian extension of K. Apply global Artin
reciprocity to the principal idele y of K in that extension.
At a finite place v off ell,q, either x kills D_v, or there is
an inertia-fixed r with y/r^m a unit. In the second alternative,
r belongs to the fixed field of absolute inertia of the chosen
place over K, the maximal unramified extension of the henselian
local field inside the chosen algebraic closure. Its value group,
normalized by ord_v of a K-uniformizer =1, is Z. More explicitly
take a finite extension of the henselization containing r in that
fixed field: ramification index one, so ord_v(r) is integral.
Hence ord_v(y)=m ord_v(r) is an integer multiple of m and its
contribution to f vanishes. Do not assume r is a global K-element
or use the Q-normalized valuation away from the completely split
prime. The native off-q hypothesis is exactly sufficient.

At q=1 mod Nm, q splits completely in K. Its places have
representatives s_u^{-1}B, u in (Z/Nm)^*. At the chosen B,
ord_B(q)=1 is also the K-normalization. Thus k_u=ord_B(s_u y)
are integers, and their arithmetic Frobenius elements are
s_u^{-1} phi s_u. Conjugation gives x(s_u^{-1} phi s_u)
=chi(s_u)x(phi); on m-torsion this scalar is u^e psi(u).
All other principal idele contributions are zero. We obtain
    (sum_u k_u u^e psi(u)) (a^e-1) x(phi)=0.        (A)
Multiplying (A) by 2 yields the exact native Artin statement,
with the SAME k_u and no division by 2. The native class-field,
inertia surjectivity, and splitting inputs remain explicit.
A uniform inversion of all Artin maps is harmless, not a
change of sign at only one local place.

